.TH MKTEMP 1 2026-09-28 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mktemp \- create a temporary file or directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mktemp
.RB [ \-dqu ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.RI [ template ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mktemp
creates a file, or with
.B \-d
a directory, whose name is
.I template
with its trailing run of
.B X
characters replaced by random letters and digits, and writes the name
to standard output.
The template must end in at least three
.BR X s;
more make collisions less likely.
.PP
The file is created with mode 0600 and the directory with mode 0700,
both owned by the caller, and creation is exclusive: an existing name
is never reused.
If a generated name exists, another is tried.
.PP
With no
.IR template ,
.B mktemp
uses
.B tmp.XXXXXXXXXX
in the directory named by
.BR TMPDIR ,
or
.B /tmp
if
.B TMPDIR
is unset or empty.
Otherwise the template is taken as given, relative to the current
directory, unless
.B \-p
or
.B \-t
is used.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Create a directory instead of a file.
.TP
.BI \-p " dir"
Create the name in
.IR dir .
If
.I dir
is empty,
.B TMPDIR
or
.B /tmp
is used.
.TP
.B \-q
Fail silently: print no error messages.
.TP
.B \-t
Create the name in
.B TMPDIR
if it is set and non-empty, else in the
.B \-p
directory, else in
.BR /tmp .
.TP
.B \-u
Unsafe: print a name that did not exist when it was checked, but
create nothing.
Another process can take the name before the caller uses it; prefer
creating the file with
.B mktemp
itself.
.PP
With
.B \-p
or
.BR \-t ,
.I template
must be a single name component, without
.BR / .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file or directory was created (or, with
.BR \-u ,
a name was found) and its name printed.
.TP
.B 1
An option or the template was invalid, the file or directory could not
be created, or no unused name was found.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Directory for the default template,
.BR \-t ,
and an empty
.B \-p
argument.
.SH EXAMPLES
Use a scratch file and remove it when the script exits:
.PP
.EX
tmp=$(mktemp) || exit 1
trap 'rm \-f "$tmp"' EXIT
sort data > "$tmp"
.EE
.PP
Create a scratch directory named after the script:
.PP
.EX
dir=$(mktemp \-d \-t myscript.XXXXXX) || exit 1
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR mkdtemp (3),
.BR mkstemp (3),
.BR tmpfile (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.B mktemp
is not in POSIX.
This implementation follows GNU coreutils: the default template, at
least three
.BR X s,
and the
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-q ,
.B \-t
and
.B \-u
options.
Unlike the BSD
.BR mktemp ,
.B \-t
takes no prefix argument, and only one template is accepted.
